const IMG = '/images/3-days-in-lima-itinerary';
---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    route={['Peru', 'Lima', 'South America']}
    label="Guide · Eight Experiences"
    title="Best Things to Do in Lima"
    standfirst="Lima contains several cities at once: a colonial capital, a Pacific city above the cliffs, neighbourhoods made for walking, and an older archaeological landscape beneath the modern one."
    stats={[
      { num: '8', label: 'Experiences' },
      { num: '3', label: 'Route days' },
      { num: '5', label: 'Lima districts' },
      { num: '1', label: 'City' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Lima is not one attraction or one district. The Historic Centre gathers
          churches, civic buildings and plazas into the city’s colonial and
          political heart. Barranco turns the streets towards art, low-rise houses
          and public space. Miraflores reaches the Pacific along cliffs and coastal
          parks. Archaeological sites rise inside modern districts, while museums
          hold cultures that existed long before the Spanish capital.
        </p>
        <p>
          The best things to do in Lima are therefore connected by contrast rather
          than by one efficient sightseeing loop. A church can lead to a square, a
          neighbourhood can open onto the ocean, and a modern apartment district can
          contain the remains of a much older city. The pleasure is in watching those
          versions occupy the same map.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the full chronology, use the <a href="/3-days-in-lima-itinerary/">three-day Lima itinerary</a>. This guide stays with the experiences that explain Lima most clearly on a first visit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <AtAGlance
      id="lima-glance"
      title="Lima at a Glance"
      meta={[
        { label: 'Best first stop', value: 'The Historic Centre for Plaza Mayor, civic architecture and the colonial structure of the capital.' },
        { label: 'Best neighbourhood', value: 'Barranco for walking, art, low-rise streets and a slower urban rhythm.' },
        { label: 'Best coastal experience', value: 'Miraflores and the Malecón, where Lima meets the Pacific along the cliffs.' },
        { label: 'Best historical depth', value: 'Huaca Pucllana, Huaca Huallamarca and Museo Larco for the pre-Hispanic city beneath modern Lima.' },
      ]}
      daysHeading="Five Ways into Lima"
      days={[
        { num: 'Historic Lima', detail: 'Plaza Mayor · Churches · Civic power', text: 'The colonial and political centre of the city, where religious institutions, public squares and government buildings explain Lima’s formal history.' },
        { num: 'Barranco', detail: 'Streets · Art · Museums · Neighbourhood life', text: 'A district best experienced on foot, where houses, galleries, public space and the descent towards the coast accumulate gradually.' },
        { num: 'Pacific Lima', detail: 'Miraflores · Malecón · Cliffs', text: 'The coastal edge changes the scale of the city. Parks and paths run above the Pacific while modern Lima continues immediately behind them.' },
        { num: 'Archaeological Lima', detail: 'Pucllana · Huallamarca · Older city', text: 'Pre-Hispanic sites survive inside modern districts, making Lima’s older layers impossible to separate neatly from the present city.' },
        { num: 'Museum Lima', detail: 'Museo Larco · MALI · Collections', text: 'Museums give chronology to the ruins, architecture and objects encountered outside, connecting ancient Peru with colonial and modern Lima.' },
      ]}
    />

    <Chapter id="historic-centre" number="I." name="Explore Lima’s Historic Centre" epithet="Colonial streets, civic power and the religious heart of the capital" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The Historic Centre is the natural beginning because Lima’s public history is
        concentrated here. Plaza Mayor gathers the Cathedral, the Government Palace,
        the Archbishop’s Palace and the surrounding civic buildings into one formal
        space. The square is not merely a backdrop for façades. It explains how Lima
        was established as the political and religious centre of Spanish South
        America.
      </p>
      <p>
        The surrounding walk adds different forms of authority. The Basilica and
        Convent of San Francisco brings the city’s religious history into the route;
        Santo Domingo extends that history through another major complex; Las
        Nazarenas keeps devotion present in a more intimate urban setting. Municipal
        buildings and government walls return the walk to the modern capital.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point is not to count churches. It is to see how the colonial city was
        organised around public squares, religious institutions and administrative
        power. The dedicated <a href="/lima-historic-centre-san-francisco-catacombs/">Historic Centre and San Francisco guide</a> follows that central walk in more detail.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/lima-plaza-mayor.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/lima-plaza-mayor-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/lima-plaza-mayor-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/lima-plaza-mayor-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/lima-plaza-mayor-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/lima-plaza-mayor.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Plaza Mayor at the civic heart of historic Lima."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Plaza Mayor places Lima’s civic and religious history in one formal centre."
      priority
    />

    <Chapter id="barranco" number="II." name="Spend Time in Barranco" epithet="A neighbourhood where streets, art, houses and museums accumulate" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Barranco rewards walking because its character is spread across a cluster of
        small transitions. The Bridge of Sighs leads towards the Bajada de Baños;
        the route passes public gardens, painted walls and low-rise architecture
        before reaching museums such as MATE and Museo Pedro de Osma. Nothing needs
        to announce itself as the single reason to come. Barranco works through the
        accumulation of the walk.
      </p>
      <p>
        The neighbourhood also changes the pace of Lima. The Historic Centre is
        shaped by civic and religious institutions. Barranco is read through the
        relationship between houses, streets, galleries and the descent towards the
        coast. Its museums are stronger when they remain part of that neighbourhood
        rather than replacing it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Give Barranco time to be a district, not a short stop at the Bridge of Sighs.
        The <a href="/barranco-to-miraflores-lima/">Barranco to Miraflores guide</a> continues the coastal movement without pretending the two neighbourhoods are identical.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/lima-barranco-street-art.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/lima-barranco-street-art-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/lima-barranco-street-art-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/lima-barranco-street-art-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/lima-barranco-street-art-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/lima-barranco-street-art.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Street art and a passage in Barranco, Lima."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Barranco combines street art, low-rise architecture and public space."
    />

    <Chapter id="miraflores" number="III." name="Walk the Miraflores Coast" epithet="Cliffs, parks and the Pacific edge of the city" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Miraflores changes Lima from a dense urban experience into a city visibly
        suspended above the Pacific. The Malecón runs along the cliffs through parks
        and open viewpoints, while Love Park gives the coastal walk a recognisable
        centre. The scale changes quickly: apartments, traffic and commercial streets
        remain close behind, but the land ends dramatically ahead.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not primarily a beach recommendation. The coast matters because it
        shows how Lima meets the ocean. From the cliffs, the Pacific is not a distant
        strip beyond the city but one of its defining edges. Barranco approaches the
        coast through neighbourhood streets and a descent; Miraflores holds the city
        above it in a longer line of parks and paths.
      </p>
      <p>
        Leave room to walk rather than treating the Malecón as a viewpoint to tick
        off between transfers. The coast is one of the experiences that gives Lima
        its geography.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="pucllana" number="IV." name="Visit Huaca Pucllana" epithet="Pre-Hispanic Lima rising inside modern Miraflores" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Huaca Pucllana is one of Lima’s strongest juxtapositions. A large adobe
        ceremonial centre of the Lima culture, built roughly between 450 and 650 CE,
        rises inside modern Miraflores. Apartment buildings, traffic and restaurants
        surround a structure made from thousands of hand-formed bricks and organised
        around platforms, plazas and enclosures.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is important because it refuses the idea that archaeology belongs
        outside the contemporary city. Pucllana is not a remote ruin that needs a
        separate historical frame. It is part of the district’s present geography.
        The Lima culture built here centuries before the Spanish foundation of Lima;
        later occupation, research and conservation have kept that earlier city in
        view.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/huaca-pucllana-lima/">Huaca Pucllana guide</a> stays with the site itself. On a first visit, place it after the Miraflores coast if the day can hold both the modern Pacific edge and the older ground beneath the district.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/lima-huaca-pucllana.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/lima-huaca-pucllana-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/lima-huaca-pucllana-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/lima-huaca-pucllana-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/lima-huaca-pucllana-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/lima-huaca-pucllana.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Huaca Pucllana archaeological complex inside modern Miraflores."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Huaca Pucllana survives inside modern Miraflores."
    />

    <Chapter id="huallamarca" number="V." name="See Huaca Huallamarca" epithet="Another archaeological layer beneath San Isidro’s modern district" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Huaca Huallamarca makes the archaeological story wider. In San Isidro, the
        site stands among one of Lima’s modern districts, where offices, apartment
        buildings and broad roads make the older structure easy to overlook. The
        archaeological mound is associated with successive occupations and later
        funerary use by cultures including Lima, Ichma and Chancay.
      </p>
      <p>
        Seeing Huallamarca after Pucllana changes the meaning of the first site.
        Pucllana is not an isolated survival inside Miraflores. Pre-Hispanic Lima
        sits beneath several modern districts, and each huaca gives a different edge
        to the same historical fact. The city has been rebuilt over older cities
        without fully erasing them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="larco" number="VI." name="Visit Museo Larco" epithet="Archaeological fragments arranged into a history of ancient Peru" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Museo Larco is where the archaeological day becomes chronological. Founded
        by Rafael Larco Hoyle in 1926, the museum brings together a large collection
        of pre-Columbian objects, including ceramics, textiles, metals and works from
        cultures across ancient Peru. Its strength is not one famous gallery. It is
        the way objects are arranged so that fragments begin to form a sequence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Larco’s ceramics are especially useful after seeing the huacas. At Pucllana
        and Huallamarca, the past appears as architecture in the middle of the city.
        In the museum, vessels and other objects show the societies that made and
        used those spaces. The collection reaches beyond the Inca, giving the older
        history of Peru more depth than a short visit to the best-known imperial
        sites can provide.
      </p>
      <p>
        The museum’s gold and silver collections, textiles and erotic ceramics add
        different registers without turning the visit into a catalogue. Museo Larco
        earns its place because it gives chronology to the archaeological ground
        already encountered outside.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="museums" number="VII." name="Spend Time in Lima’s Museums" epithet="MALI and the central collections that widen the city’s chronology" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Museum time matters in Lima because the city’s outdoor layers are too large
        to explain on their own. MALI, in the Palacio de la Exposición, provides the
        broadest frame: its collections move through pre-Columbian, colonial,
        republican and modern art. After the Historic Centre and the huacas, that
        range gives the city’s changes a longer chronology.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Museo de Minerales Andrés del Castillo offers a different material
        emphasis, while the parks and civic spaces around central Lima keep the day
        connected to the streets. Choose the collection that most interests you
        rather than trying to make every museum carry equal weight. Indoor time is
        useful here not as shelter from the city, but as a way of understanding what
        the buildings and archaeological sites outside have already begun to show.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/lima-museum-interior.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/lima-museum-interior-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/lima-museum-interior-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/lima-museum-interior-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/lima-museum-interior-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/lima-museum-interior.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Museum interior from Lima’s central cultural district."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Museum time gives Lima’s streets and archaeological sites a longer chronology."
    />

    <Chapter id="between" number="VIII." name="Walk the City Between the Landmarks" epithet="The transitions where Lima’s different versions meet" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Lima’s transitions deserve their own place in the itinerary. Colonial streets
        open onto Plaza Mayor. Barranco’s low-rise streets descend towards the coast.
        Miraflores changes from apartments and commerce to cliffs and parks. Huaca
        Pucllana sits beside modern roads and buildings; museums return the visitor
        to the city after the archaeological sites.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this needs to become a single continuous walking route. The city is
        too spread out for that claim to be useful. What matters is the movement
        between registers: the ordinary streets, short transfers, public parks and
        neighbourhood edges that keep the headline attractions from becoming
        isolated scenes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Allow time for those spaces. Lima is not only the Plaza Mayor, the Bridge of
        Sighs, the Malecón or the huacas. The city between them is where the layers
        stop behaving like separate attractions and begin to belong to one place.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/lima-street-vendor.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/lima-street-vendor-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/lima-street-vendor-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/lima-street-vendor-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/lima-street-vendor-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/lima-street-vendor.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Street vendor and everyday movement in Lima."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="The city between the landmarks is part of Lima’s story."
    />

    <Practical place="Lima — Things to Do" detail="Eight experiences · three days" detailIcon="🧭">
      <PracticalSection label="Shortest route to a great first visit" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>One day</strong> — Start with the Historic Centre, then choose either Barranco plus the coast or Barranco plus one archaeological stop.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Two days</strong> — Give the Historic Centre its own day, then combine Barranco, Miraflores and Huaca Pucllana on the second.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Three days</strong> — Follow the <a href="/3-days-in-lima-itinerary/">full Lima itinerary</a> so the central museums, coastal neighbourhoods and archaeological sites are not compressed into one oversized route.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>If you only choose three things</strong> — Historic Centre, Barranco and Huaca Pucllana or the Miraflores coast depending on whether history or geography matters more.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
      <PracticalSection label="What each part of Lima does best" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Colonial and civic history:</strong> Historic Centre.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Neighbourhood character:</strong> Barranco.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Pacific geography:</strong> Miraflores coast.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Pre-Hispanic archaeology:</strong> Huaca Pucllana and Huaca Huallamarca.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Historical context:</strong> Museo Larco and MALI.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
      <PracticalSection label="Watch-outs" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Do not treat Lima as one walkable centre.</strong> The city is spread across several districts, so transfers are part of the itinerary.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Give Barranco time to be a neighbourhood.</strong> Do not reduce it to a Bridge of Sighs photo stop.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Do not overload the museum day.</strong> Choose collections that genuinely add context rather than trying to clear every museum.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Keep archaeology inside the modern city story.</strong> The huacas are strongest when understood as older Lima surviving inside Miraflores and San Isidro.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="priorities" rule={false}>What to prioritise with one, two or three days</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        <strong>With one day:</strong> choose the Historic Centre plus either
        Barranco and the coast or Barranco and the archaeology, depending on pace.
        Historic Lima, neighbourhood Lima and the Pacific edge make a rich but
        ambitious day; there is no need to force every museum and huaca into it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With two days:</strong> give the first day to the Historic Centre.
        Use the second for Barranco, Miraflores and Huaca Pucllana, with Museo Larco
        as the stronger addition when archaeology matters more than another coastal
        stop. Huaca Huallamarca can extend that archaeological thread.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With three days:</strong> follow the <a href="/3-days-in-lima-itinerary/">complete Lima itinerary</a>, which separates the central museum day from the Barranco, Miraflores and archaeological route instead of treating the city as one compressed list.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="closing" rule={false}>The city beneath the city</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Lima does not resolve itself into one image. The colonial centre, Barranco’s
        streets, the Pacific cliffs and the huacas belong to different centuries, but
        they occupy the same city. Museo Larco and MALI give those layers chronology;
        ordinary streets and public parks keep them connected to the present.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pleasure of Lima is discovering how often one version survives beside
        another. A modern district can carry an older city beneath it. A road can
        open from civic stone to ocean. A museum can turn a ruin into a history that
        continues beyond the walls. The <a href="/3-days-in-lima-itinerary/">three-day route</a> is the natural next step when those layers need to become a journey.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="beat">But the road is ever onward.</p>

    <FurtherReading
      label="Continue through Lima"
      cards={[
        { href: '/3-days-in-lima-itinerary/', region: 'Peru · Lima', title: '3 Days in Lima: Complete Itinerary', excerpt: 'The full route through the Historic Centre, Barranco, Miraflores, archaeology and museums.', cta: 'READ CHRONICLE' },
        { href: '/barranco-to-miraflores-lima/', region: 'Peru · Lima', title: 'Barranco to Miraflores', excerpt: 'A focused coastal movement through Barranco, the Malecón and Miraflores.', cta: 'READ CHRONICLE' },
        { href: '/huaca-pucllana-lima/', region: 'Peru · Lima', title: 'Huaca Pucllana', excerpt: 'A closer look at the pre-Hispanic ceremonial centre surviving inside modern Miraflores.', cta: 'READ CHRONICLE' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>
  <ArticleFAQ page="best-things-to-do-in-lima" />
</ChronicleLayout>
